24	Retrain-chain experiments (LoRA fine-tuning each generation) yield $\Kempirical{=}7$ for both Qwen-1.5B and Pythia-1.4B (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), consistent across architectures despite a ${\sim}3\times$ difference in LoRA effective capacity.
25	The gap arises because each LoRA fine-tuning step introduces cumulative model artifacts (vocabulary narrowing, repetition bias) that persist as additional depth signal; these artifacts are setup-specific and unlikely to generalize to uncontrolled contamination.
26	Prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$ is thus the operationally relevant floor for data curation; retrain-chain $\Kempirical{=}7$ represents the upper bound under controlled conditions.
27	OLMo retrain results are pending; if $\Kempirical{\geq}4$, this would confirm the pattern across three architecture families.
28	
29	\paragraph{JSD decay, domain dependence, and the two-tier ceiling.}
30	Token-level JSD reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 exhibits a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.9$), arXiv decays $3\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$), and the retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$, $\Kempirical{=}7$; Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).
